<Wiki
  title="puv lock"
  description="Resolve the manifest and write puv.lock. --upgrade ignores versions already locked."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>lock</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv lock</code> resolves and writes <code>puv.lock</code>. It does not, by itself, mean
    “install”. Install the file you just wrote with
    <a href="/wiki/commands/sync"><code>puv sync</code></a> or
    <a href="/wiki/commands/install"><code>puv install</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep locked versions that still fit</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv lock</pre>
  <p>
    The solver picks the highest stable release, and it prefers a version already in the lock
    when that version still satisfies the manifest. Adding a package with
    <a href="/wiki/commands/add"><code>puv add</code></a> already locks. You run <code>puv lock</code>
    when you edited <code>puv.toml</code> yourself, or when
    <code>sync</code> stopped because the manifest drifted.
  </p>
  <pre class="block"># edit [dependencies] in puv.toml
puv sync
# stops and tells you to lock
puv lock
puv sync</pre>

  <h2>Ignore the lock</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv lock --upgrade</pre>
  <p>
    <code>--upgrade</code> resolves again and ignores locked versions. A constraint of
    <code>^7.0</code> can move from 7.0.1 to 7.4.2. It cannot move to 8.x, because that is
    outside the constraint. Upgrades that fit, versus newer releases that do not, are also what
    <a href="/wiki/commands/audit"><code>puv audit</code></a> lists.
  </p>
  <p>
    The lock stores the SHA-256 of each archive, and <code>dist.shasum</code> when Packagist sent
    one. Details are on <a href="/wiki/resolution">resolution</a>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
